# Real TLS termination for pulsewatch's operator-facing surface (R-001,
# 08-deployment-and-operations.md). Terminates HTTPS for the browser-facing
# frontend and, for direct API/curl use, the backend — neither is reachable
# in plain HTTP from the host any more (see docker-compose.yml: `frontend`
# no longer publishes a host port; `backend`'s own host port stays published
# separately for the agent's REST/OTLP traffic, ADR-0003, untouched by this
# change).
#
# `tls internal` issues a certificate from Caddy's own local CA (stored in
# the `caddy_data` volume, so it's stable across restarts, not regenerated
# every `docker compose up`) rather than a publicly trusted one — a real,
# self-signed cert, not a placeholder. A real browser will show a
# not-trusted warning for it; that is the accepted, named trade-off for the
# self-hosted/local-default case documented in
# 08-deployment-and-operations.md. Anyone exposing this deployment on a real
# public domain should replace this file's `tls internal` with `tls {
# operator@yourdomain }` (or just the site address as the domain) to get a
# real Let's Encrypt certificate instead — see that doc for the full
# instructions.
{
	auto_https disable_redirects
}

# Explicit hostnames, not a bare `:443` — Caddy's `tls internal` only
# pre-provisions a certificate for names it's told to manage; a portless/
# hostless address has no name to provision one for, and would refuse
# every TLS handshake with "no certificate available" regardless of SNI.
# `localhost`/`127.0.0.1` matches how this is actually reached (a browser
# on the same host as `docker compose up`, per this project's dev/self-
# hosted default — see 08-deployment-and-operations.md for the real-domain
# alternative).
localhost, 127.0.0.1 {
	tls internal

	handle /api/* {
		reverse_proxy backend:8080
	}

	handle {
		reverse_proxy frontend:3000
	}
}
